<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       id="load-time-weaving" title="Load-time weaving">

    <show-structure for="chapter,procedure" depth="2"/>

    <tldr>
        <p>Needs a module compiled with the processor from <a href="install.topic">Install</a>. The
            agent reads the manifest that compilation wrote, never your sources.</p>
    </tldr>

    <p>
        The agent rewrites each class as the JVM defines it. Nothing on disk changes. Every jar you
        deploy keeps the bytes it was published with.
    </p>

    <code-block lang="bash">
java -javaagent:lib/aether-weaver-agent-%version%.jar \
     -cp "app.jar:lib/*" \
     app.Main
    </code-block>

    <chapter title="The jars the flag needs" id="classpath">
        <p>
            That agent jar holds four classes plus <code>package-info</code>. Its manifest names one
            of them twice: <code>Premain-Class</code> and <code>Agent-Class</code> both point to
            <code>WeaverAgent</code>. It calls into the api directly, as well as
            <code>aether-weaver-runtime</code> and the engine below. Declare the agent at
            <code>runtime</code> scope. The procedure below fills <path>lib</path> with all four
            jars; <a href="artifacts.topic">Artefacts and modules</a> has the graph.
        </p>

        <warning>
            <p>
                <code>-jar app.jar</code> ignores <code>-cp</code>. The agent then starts with none
                of those jars. The JVM dies before <code>main</code> with a
                <code>NoClassDefFoundError</code>. Three <code>java.lang.instrument ASSERTION
                FAILED</code> lines come first. Then <code>FATAL ERROR in native method: processing
                of -javaagent failed, processJavaStart failed</code>. It exits with status 134. Name
                your main class on a <code>-cp</code> command line instead.
            </p>
        </warning>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Where the transformer goes in" id="startup">
        <code-block lang="mermaid">
flowchart TB
    A["JVM starts"] --> B["premain resolves options"]
    B --> C["Manifests searched for weaves"]
    C --> D["Transformer installed, plan counted on one line"]
    D --> E["Application main begins"]
    E --> F{"Does a weave claim this class?"}
    F -->|yes| G["Rewritten bytes defined instead"]
    F -->|no| H["Original bytes kept"]
    G --> I["Diagnostics collected, nothing prints them"]
        </code-block>

        <p>
            Classes the JVM defined before <code>premain</code> returned are never revisited. Under
            this flag those are JDK and %product% classes, never your own. Attaching to a running
            JVM instead is <a href="runtime-weaving.topic">Weaving in a running application</a>.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <procedure title="Weave an application at load time" id="run">
        <step>
            <p>Add the agent beside the two dependencies you already declare. It brings the runtime
                and the engine with it.</p>
            <code-block lang="xml"><![CDATA[
<dependency>
    <groupId>%group%</groupId>
    <artifactId>aether-weaver-agent</artifactId>
    <scope>runtime</scope>
</dependency>
]]></code-block>
        </step>
        <step>
            <p>Bind <code>copy-dependencies</code> so <path>lib</path> holds those jars before you
                run anything.</p>
            <code-block lang="xml"><![CDATA[
<plugin>
    <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
    <artifactId>maven-dependency-plugin</artifactId>
    <executions>
        <execution>
            <phase>package</phase>
            <goals><goal>copy-dependencies</goal></goals>
            <configuration>
                <outputDirectory>lib</outputDirectory>
                <includeScope>runtime</includeScope>
            </configuration>
        </execution>
    </executions>
</plugin>
]]></code-block>
        </step>
        <step>
            <p>Start the application with the command above. The agent's first line is your
                confirmation. It counts the plan, and it names the entry point that installed
                it.</p>
            <code-block lang="console"><![CDATA[
Aether Weaver %version% — 1 weave, 1 target, fingerprint <64 hex characters>, enabled, verification=strict, onError=fail, phase=default (premain).
]]></code-block>
        </step>
        <step>
            <p>Ask for the plan itself when a count is not enough. Add
                <code>=explain=true</code> to the flag.</p>
            <code-block lang="console">
  app.Greeting  [INSTANCE, priority 0, origin: weave manifest (app.jar)]
    → app.Target
        INJECT   onGreet()   → greet() @HEAD   not woven yet
            </code-block>
        </step>
        <step>
            <p>Read <code>not woven yet</code> as written. The report prints while the agent
                installs. No target of yours has loaded by then. So it is complete about the plan
                and silent about matches. <a href="agent-options.topic#entry-points">Agent
                options</a> compares the two entry points.</p>
        </step>
    </procedure>

    <chapter title="When the closing line says something else" id="trouble">
        <deflist type="full">
            <def title="no weaves to apply">
                <p>
                    <code>AW2302</code> prints first, three lines above the closing line. It
                    appears when no <path>META-INF/aether/weaves.json</path> is on the classpath.
                    Add <code>aether-weaver-processor</code> to every module that declares a weave,
                    and rebuild. <a href="weave-discovery.topic">How a weave is found</a> follows
                    the file.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="disabled by configuration">
                <p>
                    Something set <code>enabled=false</code>, on the flag or as a system property.
                    <a href="configuration.topic#prove">Prove which layer decided it</a>.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Nothing at all">
                <p>
                    The flag never reached this JVM. A missing <code>-javaagent:</code> path fails
                    loudly instead, so silence means the flag was dropped. Check whatever wrapper or
                    start script builds the command.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="After that line the agent stops talking" id="quiet">
        <p>
            The diagnostic list is drained only at the four points that end installation. A weave
            that fails once the application is running lands on that list afterwards. So does a
            class that arrives carrying another plan's record. Nothing reads either one again.
            <a href="agent-options.topic#output">Agent options</a> lists what that loses. It also
            has what <code>onError=fail</code> prints before it halts the JVM.
        </p>

        <note>
            <p>
                Weave the same classes at build time instead when printed diagnostics matter more.
                Running both drivers on the same classes raises a shared weave twice, as
                <code>AW2202</code>. Matching the two plans, or dropping one driver, stops it.
                <a href="choose-a-driver.topic#both">Running two of them</a> has the rule, and
                <a href="build-time-weaving.topic">Build-time weaving</a> is the pom.
            </p>
        </note>
    </chapter>

